  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on a sample of literature restricted to the Scopus database and note a heavy reliance on quantitative methods, which limits deeper qualitative insights into how transit perceptions are formed.",
      "what": "This systematic literature review analyzed 67 empirical articles from the Scopus database published between 2004 and 2025 to synthesize how perceived accessibility of public transport is measured and understood. The analysis mapped the relationships between perceived accessibility, transit use, and activity participation, identifying how individual, land-use, transport, and temporal factors are integrated.",
      "why": "The review reveals that existing research frequently isolates individual factors rather than examining their interaction with spatiotemporal constraints, and systematically overlooks intra-group differences in perception among vulnerable populations like young adults and low-income individuals who face transport-related social exclusion."
    }
  },
